Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Riemannian Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Riemannian Infrageometry",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " studies how to measure and distinguish graphs as geometric substrates. In \
contrast, synthetic infrageometry asks only whether certain assumptions are \
satisfied by the substrate so that constructions have given propertes studies \
constructions and relations of geometric objects within a substrate whose \
properties are fixed by postulates. Riemann began with the problem of \
measuring the length of curves. He viewed an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "899f389d-e982-4f77-850b-127f1e6df014"],
 "-dimensional manifold as an iterated ",
 StyleBox["infinitesimal webbing of paths",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and asked what conditions to impose on the infinitesimal element of \
length. Restricting it to a quadratic form gives the ",
 StyleBox["metric tensor",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", which captures the infinitesimal Riemannian geometry. This separates the \
manifold and its topology from the Riemannian structure placed on it. One can \
then ask for the moduli space of Riemannian structures on a fixed manifold, \
or go the other way and obtain topological quantities by integrating local \
geometric quantities, as in the Gauss\[Dash]Bonnet theorem. In infrageometry \
the discrete ",
 StyleBox["webbing of paths",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " is the substrate itself. The graph prescribes both the paths and their \
metric, so this split is not made. But we must always choose an ",
 StyleBox["observer scale",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "08f121be-49cf-4784-a925-6922fb8c10db"],
 ", which can be understood as measuring the substrate using balls of radius ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "c3725240-ba75-4426-af64-44fa59c7d2cd"],
 ". A Gromov\[Dash]Hausdorff limit of rescaled graphs, when it exists, gives \
a metric space. If this metric space is a smooth Riemannian manifold, its \
intrinsic distance determines its Riemannian structure. Thus two such \
manifolds that are isometric as metric spaces are also isometric as \
Riemannian manifolds. The geometry is here ",
 StyleBox["in one",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

